From b908b041486b397d2849f3484e35e1b0bda33c0e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 09:34:52 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 083/119] scene-referred S4: our own Rec.709-authored colours,
 in the working primaries

S3 left this deliberately undone so it could be seen on its own (STRUCTURE.md 7b, "still
outstanding"): every colour our optics carry was picked by eye against a Rec.709 picture, and
since S3 those numbers have been multiplying AP1 data. A 709-encoded orange used as an AP1
multiplier is a slightly different orange - a small, systematic, entirely silent error.

The authored values do NOT change. They stay Rec.709, because that is what the tooltip says, what
the colour picker shows and what a lens preset will carry. What changes is the moment each
becomes a multiplier of working-space data, and each has exactly one such moment:

| colour                    | converted at                                              |
|---------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|
| anamorphic coating tint    | Bloom.cpp, the stretch pass that reaches the bloom target |
| halation dye tint          | Bloom.cpp, the halation composite                         |
| diffraction spectrum (x6)  | Bloom.cpp, BEFORE the white renormalisation               |
| lens ghost tint            | DepthOfField.cpp, AFTER the Rec.709 hue fan               |
| front filter star hue (x3) | in the shader, gated by a new FilterPSFParams1.w          |

Two of those have an order that matters and is easy to get backwards. The diffraction spectrum's
six columns are rescaled so their weighted sum is exactly white; converting after that would
renormalise Rec.709 columns and then tilt the result, so the conversion goes first. The ghost
chain fans its coatings out in hue about grey, which is a Rec.709 operation on a Rec.709 colour,
so the fan happens first and only the result crosses over.

The front filter is the one authored colour that lives in a shader rather than a CPU constant, so
it gets a constant instead: FilterPSFParams1.w says whether the target is AP1 and the shader runs
Rec709ToACEScg on the three dispersion anchors. An AUTHORED PSF image is left alone - it is a
texture the user supplied and there is no knowing what primaries it was painted in; treating it as
Rec.709 would be a guess dressed as a correction.

CRendererResources::ToWorkingSpace() is the one implementation, keyed on
IsSceneReferredWorkingSpace() so the r_SceneReferredDebug 1 A/B stays coherent. The matrix is
CommonMath.cfi's to the digit; every row sums to one, so a white or grey tint maps to itself
exactly and an untinted effect is bit-identical on both paths. Off the switch the function is a
return of its argument.

The picture must not visibly move. This is the same colour expressed in different primaries.

